[1;33m[2026-03-03 16:46:47,339 [31mINFO [32magents/221-MainProcess[1;33m] [0mObservation: # Analysis Report: Vocational School Graduates Job Market

## Overview
This report analyzes job posting data for vocational school graduates. The dataset contains 9,073 job postings with information on salary, benefits, and job requirements. After filtering out records with missing salary information, 7,936 valid entries remain for analysis.

## 1. Starting Salary Distribution

### Summary Statistics
- **Mean**: 4,816 yuan/month
- **Median**: 4,500 yuan/month
- **Standard Deviation**: 1,918 yuan/month
- **Range**: 4 to 30,000 yuan/month (note extreme outliers exist)

### Frequency Distribution (Binned)
| Salary Bin | Count | Percentage |
|------------|-------|------------|
| <2k        | 24    | 0.3%       |
| 2k-3k      | 226   | 2.8%       |
| 3k-4k      | 1,866 | 23.5%      |
| 4k-5k      | 2,438 | **30.7%**  |
| 5k-6k      | 1,554 | 19.6%      |
| 6k-8k      | 1,139 | 14.4%      |
| 8k-10k     | 404   | 5.1%       |
| 10k-15k    | 238   | 3.0%       |
| 15k-20k    | 32    | 0.4%       |
| >20k       | 15    | 0.2%       |

**Insight**: The salary distribution is right-skewed, with most positions offering between 3,000 and 6,000 yuan/month. Over 74% of jobs fall in the 3k-6k range.

## 2. Benefits Distribution

We extracted and cleaned 105 unique benefit keywords from the Benefits field. The top 20 most frequently offered benefits are:

| Rank | Benefit | Frequency | % of Postings |
|------|---------|-----------|---------------|
| 1    | Five social insurances | 6,380 | 80.4% |
| 2    | Paid annual leave | 4,221 | 53.2% |
| 3    | Holiday benefits | 4,164 | 52.5% |
| 4    | Performance bonus | 3,502 | 44.1% |
| 5    | Full attendance bonus | 2,888 | 36.4% |
| 6    | Double salary at the end of the year | 2,659 | 33.5% |
| 7    | Employee travel | 2,220 | 28.0% |
| 8    | Housing provident fund | 2,166 | 27.3% |
| 9    | Professional training | 2,124 | 26.8% |
| 10   | Overtime pay | 2,011 | 25.3% |
| 11   | Meal allowance | 1,844 | 23.2% |
| 12   | Commercial insurance | 1,751 | 22.1% |
| 13   | Business trip allowance | 1,449 | 18.3% |
| 14   | Work uniform | 1,431 | 18.0% |
| 15   | High-temperature allowance | 1,343 | 16.9% |
| 16   | Meals provided | 1,273 | 16.0% |
| 17   | Multiple salary adjustments per year | 1,235 | 15.6% |
| 18   | Transportation allowance | 1,186 | 14.9% |
| 19   | Communication allowance | 1,105 | 13.9% |
| 20   | Accommodation provided | 875 | 11.0% |

**Insight**: Social insurance is nearly universal, reflecting regulatory compliance. Beyond that, performance-linked bonuses and leave benefits are common, highlighting an emphasis on rewarding attendance and extra effort.

## 3. Qualities That Significantly Increase Starting Salaries

### 3.1 Work Experience
Work experience has the strongest relationship with salary. The average salary rises consistently with each additional year of required experience.

| Experience Category | Average Salary (yuan/month) | Count |
|-------------------|----------------------------|-------|
| 6+ years          | 9,500                      | 9     |
| 8+ years          | 8,868                      | 19    |
| 10+ years         | 7,643                      | 14    |
| 5+ years          | 6,912                      | 243   |
| 4+ years          | 6,588                      | 34    |
| 3+ years          | 5,784                      | 800   |
| 2+ years          | 5,022                      | 1,037 |
| No limit          | 4,545                      | 4,033 |
| 1+ years          | 4,489                      | 1,582 |
| Fresh graduates   | 4,080                      | 155   |

> **Recommendation**: For vocational graduates, accumulating 2+ years of work experience can lift average salaries by ~500 yuan/month compared to jobs with no experience requirement. Targeting roles requiring 3+ years can lead to 20–30% higher pay.

### 3.2 Foreign Language Requirement
Positions asking for foreign language proficiency show mixed salary impacts. The highest‑paying categories are small samples, but on average, English‑required jobs offer about the same salary as jobs without any language requirement.

| Language Group | Average Salary | Count |
|----------------|----------------|-------|
| Other skill levels require proficiency | 11,000 | 2 |
| Proficiency in English required | 6,617 | 23 |
| Good Chinese proficiency required | 4,944 | 18 |
| English (any) | 4,715 | 195 |
| No explicit language requirement | 4,820 | 7,442 |

> **Insight**: Except for niche skill‑based categories, language proficiency alone does not guarantee a higher starting salary.

### 3.3 Age Requirement
There is a weak positive correlation (r = 0.191) between the maximum age in the age‑requirement range and the job’s starting salary. Jobs that accept older candidates tend to pay more, likely because they also demand more experience.

### 3.4 Gender Requirement
Postings that do not specify a gender requirement offer the highest average salary (4,843 yuan/month). Positions that explicitly request female candidates average 4,617 yuan/month, while those requesting male candidates average 4,102 yuan/month (note small sample size for male‑only roles).
- **Implication**: Employers with gender‑neutral hiring tend to pay more, perhaps because they operate in higher‑value industries or roles.

### 3.5 Work Location
Crude city extraction shows that postings located in **Xiamen, Quanzhou, and Fujian** generally offer higher average salaries (5,000–5,900 yuan/month) compared to other areas. However, city data is noisy; deeper geocoding would refine this finding.

## 4. Key Takeaways & Business Recommendations

1. **Experience Pays Off**: The single most powerful lever for vocational graduates to increase their starting salary is to gain **2+ years of work experience**. The salary premium for 3+ years is substantial (over 1,000 yuan/month above entry‑level).

2. **Benefits are Standard**: Employers overwhelmingly provide statutory benefits (social insurance). Beyond that, performance‑based incentives (bonuses, attendance rewards) are widespread, suggesting that vocational roles are often tied to measurable output.

3. **Language Skills Are Not a Major Differentiator**: English proficiency alone does not command a noticeable salary premium in this dataset. Vocational graduates should prioritize technical skills and experience over language training unless targeting very specific high‑paying niches.

4. **Gender‑Neutral Roles Pay Better**: Job seekers should look for postings without gender restrictions, as these roles appear to offer higher compensation on average.

5. **Location Matters**: Jobs in major cities (Xiamen, Quanzhou) tend to pay more, although the difference is moderate. Graduates willing to relocate could see a salary boost of ~500–1,000 yuan/month.

## 5. Limitations & Future Work
- Salary extraction relied on the lower bound of a range; commission‑based components were ignored.
- Benefits were parsed from free‑text fields, leading to some duplication (e.g., “five insurances” vs “five social insurances”).
- City names were crudely extracted; a more precise geocoding would improve location‑based insights.
- No multivariate analysis was performed; observed effects may be confounded by other factors.

---

