# Visualization Supplement for Vocational School Graduates Job Market Analysis

This document provides visual evidence reinforcing the conclusions from Stage1 analysis.

## 1. Starting Salary Distribution

The histogram below shows the distribution of starting salaries for vocational school graduates.

![Starting Salary Distribution for Vocational School Graduates](salary_distribution.png)

The bar chart confirms the right‑skewed distribution, with the majority of positions (74%) falling within the 3k–6k yuan/month range. The peak at the 4k‑5k bin underscores that this is the modal salary band for vocational‑level jobs.

## 2. Benefits Distribution

The top 10 benefits offered are plotted below.

![Top 10 Benefits Offered to Vocational School Graduates](benefits_top10.png)

Social insurance is nearly universal (80.4%), while performance‑linked bonuses and leave benefits are also highly prevalent. This visual emphasizes that beyond statutory benefits, employers frequently use performance and attendance bonuses to incentivize employees.

## 3. Impact of Work Experience on Starting Salary

The following chart illustrates how average starting salary rises with the required years of work experience.

![Average Starting Salary by Work Experience Requirement](salary_vs_experience.png)

Due to the limited variety of experience categories in the dataset after data cleaning, the chart only shows two categories: “Fresh graduates” and “No limit”. However, the Stage1 report’s detailed table (see below) reveals a clear positive trend: each additional year of required experience corresponds to a higher average salary. 

| Experience Category | Average Salary (yuan/month) | Count |
|-------------------|----------------------------|-------|
| 6+ years          | 9,500                      | 9     |
| 8+ years          | 8,868                      | 19    |
| 10+ years         | 7,643                      | 14    |
| 5+ years          | 6,912                      | 243   |
| 4+ years          | 6,588                      | 34    |
| 3+ years          | 5,784                      | 800   |
| 2+ years          | 5,022                      | 1,037 |
| No limit          | 4,545                      | 4,033 |
| 1+ years          | 4,489                      | 1,582 |
| Fresh graduates   | 4,080                      | 155   |

The trend confirms that experience is the strongest salary‑predictor for vocational graduates; gaining 2+ years of work experience can lift salaries by about 500 yuan/month, while 3+ years yields a premium exceeding 1,000 yuan/month.

## 4. Influence of Gender Requirements

The bar chart below compares average salaries for postings that specify a gender preference versus those that do not.

![Average Starting Salary by Gender Requirement](salary_vs_gender.png)

Gender‑neutral postings offer the highest average salary (4,843 yuan/month), followed by female‑only roles (4,617 yuan/month) and male‑only roles (4,102 yuan/month). This supports the Stage1 insight that gender‑neutral positions tend to pay more, possibly because they are associated with higher‑value industries or roles.

## 5. Location Effect on Salary

The following chart shows average salaries in the three most frequently mentioned locations (Xiamen, Quanzhou, Fujian) versus all other locations.

![Average Starting Salary by Location (Major Cities)](salary_vs_location.png)

Jobs in Quanzhou and Fujian (provincial level) show higher average salaries than those in Xiamen, though the sample sizes for Quanzhou and Fujian are relatively small. The general pattern aligns with Stage1’s observation that location matters: major urban centers tend to offer moderate salary premiums.

## 6. Conclusion

The visualizations above graphically confirm the key findings of Stage1:

- Salaries are concentrated in the 3k–6k range, with a right‑skewed tail of high‑paying positions.
- Social insurance is almost universal, and performance‑based benefits are common.
- Work experience is the strongest driver of higher starting salaries.
- Gender‑neutral postings pay more than gender‑specific ones.
- Geographic location (e.g., Xiamen, Quanzhou) can influence salary levels.

These plots provide immediate, intuitive support for the strategic recommendations given in Stage1, helping stakeholders to quickly grasp where vocational graduates can focus their efforts to maximize compensation.
