# Stage 2: Visual Reinforcement of Stage1 Insights

## 1. Profit Margin by Category (2022–2024)
![Profit Margin by Product Category](margin_by_category.png)

The line chart confirms that Home & Furniture exhibited the largest swing (0.77 percentage points) among all categories, with a noticeable peak in 2023 followed by a drop in 2024. This visual underscores the category’s volatility and justifies the deeper investigation in Stage1.

## 2. Home & Furniture Sales‑Share Shift
![Sales Share by Margin Tier](product_mix_shift.png)

The stacked bar shows a declining share of high‑margin products (Towels, Sofa Covers, Bed Sheets) from 52.1% in 2023 to 49.5% in 2024, while low‑margin items gained ground. This shift directly explains the 2024 margin contraction, supporting Stage1’s conclusion that product‑mix change is the primary driver of fluctuation.

## 3. Discount and Shipping‑Cost Trends
![Discount and Shipping Cost Trends](discount_shipping_trends.png)

Discount rates crept upward over the three years, whereas shipping costs fluctuated and fell slightly in 2024. The chart highlights that discount pressure—not shipping costs—coincided with the margin dip, reinforcing Stage1’s finding that pricing‑related factors contributed secondarily to the volatility.

## 4. RFM Segment Margin Evolution
![Profit Margin by RFM Segment](rfm_segment_margins.png)

Core (High RFM) customers saw a sharper margin decline in 2024 compared to Mid and Low segments, dropping from 45.2% to 44.6%. This visual evidence validates Stage1’s insight that the most valuable customers are more sensitive to the drivers identified, amplifying the impact of product‑mix and discount changes.

## 5. Engagement vs. Margin (No Correlation)
![Product Margin vs Browsing Time](margin_vs_browsing.png)

The scatter plot reveals no clear relationship between average browsing time and product‑level profit margin. This confirms Stage1’s observation that engagement metrics, such as browsing duration, do not explain margin differences, shifting the focus to product‑mix and pricing as the key levers.

## Summary
These five visualizations provide graphical support for each major conclusion drawn in Stage1: the identification of the most volatile category, the product‑mix shift as the main driver, the secondary role of rising discounts, the heightened sensitivity of core RFM customers, and the lack of correlation with browsing engagement. Together they strengthen the evidence base for the business recommendations offered earlier.
