# Stage 2: Visual Evidence for Multi-Dimensional Customer Value Assessment

## Executive Summary

This document presents visual evidence supporting the development of a multi-dimensional customer value scoring system. The current model relying solely on usage duration and frequency achieves only 68% accuracy in identifying high-value customers. The following visualizations demonstrate why additional dimensions are necessary and which ones show the most promise for improving predictive performance beyond 85%.

## 1. Limitations of the Current 2D Approach

![Current 2D Model: Usage Duration vs Frequency](duration_vs_frequency_2d.png)

**Takeaway:** The scatter plot of usage duration (minutes) against event frequency reveals significant overlap in the customer base, with many accounts clustered in similar regions regardless of their NPS ratings (color-coded). This visual noise explains why a simple 2D model fails to distinguish high-value customers effectively—customers with similar engagement levels can have vastly different satisfaction scores.

**Why it matters for Stage1 conclusions:** This directly supports Stage1's finding that the current model's 68% accuracy ceiling stems from insufficient feature dimensionality. High-value customers are not just about raw usage—they exhibit distinct patterns across multiple dimensions that this 2D view cannot capture.

## 2. Distribution Analysis Reveals Opportunities

