Action: Bash(code="python3 -c \"open('stage1.md','w').write('# Risk Analytics Report: Deteriorating Customer Payment Behavior\n\n## 1. High-Risk Customer Identification\n\nWe identified high-risk customers using the following criteria:\n- Payment Rate Percentage < 75%\n- Outstanding Balance > $15,000\n\n**Result:** 399 out of 2,800 total customers (14.25%) meet the high-risk criteria.\n\n## 2. Composite Risk Score Calculation\n\nFor each high-risk customer we computed a composite risk score using the formula:\n```\nRisk Score = (100 - Payment Rate) * 0.4 + (850 - Credit Score) / 850 * 100 * 0.4 + (100 - Business Stability Score) * 0.2\n```\n\n- **Risk Score Distribution - All Customers:**\n  - Mean: 16.3, Std: 17.3, Min: 0.6, Max: 76.7\n- **High-Risk Customers:**\n  - Mean: 53.6, Std: 9.8, Min: 29.4, Max: 76.7\n- **Normal Customers:**\n  - Mean: 10.1, Std: 7.8, Min: 0.6, Max: 74.3\n\nThe scoring clearly separates the two groups, confirming that the selection criteria capture customers with genuinely higher credit and stability risk.\n\n## 3. Profit Contribution of High-Risk Customers\n\n- Total Gross Profit (all customers): -$2,501.5 M (i.e., a net loss)\n- Gross Profit contributed by high-risk customers: -$345.5 M\n- **Proportion of total loss attributable to high-risk customers:** 13.8%\n\nAlthough high-risk customers represent 14.3% of the customer base, they account for 13.8% of the total losses, indicating that their loss-generating intensity is slightly lower than that of the average customer.\n\n## 4. Collection-Rate Trend Analysis (Last 12 Months)\n\n| Month       | Collection Rate (%) | Change (p.p.) |\n|-------------|---------------------|---------------|\n| Oct-2024   | 47.11              | -             |\n| Nov-2024   | 52.05              | +4.94         |\n| Dec-2024   | 50.58              | -1.47         |\n| Jan-2025   | 48.64              | -1.94         |\n| Feb-2025   | 47.32              | -1.32         |\n| Mar-2025   | 47.85              | +0.53         |\n| Apr-2025   | 50.59              | +2.74         |\n| May-2025   | 47.64              | -2.95         |\n| Jun-2025   | 51.54              | +3.90         |\n| Jul-2025   | 50.97              | -0.57         |\n| Aug-2025   | 46.06              | -4.91         |\n| Sep-2025   | 45.76              | -0.30         |\n\n**Key Findings:**\n- **Consecutive months of deterioration:** We observed three distinct episodes of declining collection rates: a 3-month decline (Dec-2024 -> Feb-2025), a 1-month drop (May-2025), and a 3-month drop (Jul-2025 -> Sep-2025).\n- **Longest decline streak:** 3 months, covering both the Dec-Feb and Jul-Sep periods.\n- **Magnitude of decline:** The total sum of negative month-over-month changes over the 12-month window is -13.46 percentage points, with an average monthly decline of -1.92 percentage points.\n\nThe recurring 3-month downtrends signal an embedded systemic weakness in the collection process.\n\n## 5. Profile Comparison: High-Risk vs. Normal Customers\n\n| Metric                   | High-Risk (N=399)        | Normal (N=2,401)         | Difference |\n|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|------------|\n| Customer Lifespan (days)  | Mean: 911.8, Median: 894  | Mean: 931.8, Median: 940  | -2.1%      |\n| Total Invoices            | Mean: 38.4,  Median: 29   | Mean: 39.5,  Median: 30   | -2.8%      |\n| Avg. Invoice Amount ($)   | Mean: 20,816, Median: 15,714 | Mean: 20,326, Median: 15,365 | +2.4%      |\n| **Profitability Tier** (modal) | Loss Making: 99.5%              | Loss Making: 99.8%              | -0.3 pp    |\n\n**Insights:**\n- High-risk customers are marginally newer and issue slightly fewer invoices.\n- Their average invoice amount is slightly higher, suggesting larger-value transactions.\n- The overwhelming majority of both groups are \\'Loss Making\\'; however, high-risk customers have a modestly higher share of \\'Break Even\\' invoices (0.50% vs. 0.17%).\n\nThe similarity in operational and profitability profiles suggests that payment-risk is not a function of size or longevity, but is likely driven by customer-specific credit and collection dynamics.\n\n## 6. Risk-Tier Segmentation & Loss Exposure\n\nUsing the composite risk score, we segmented high-risk customers into four tiers:\n\n| Risk Tier   | Count | Percent of High-Risk | Expected Loss ($) |\n|-------------|-------|----------------------|-------------------|\n| Low         | 154   | 38.6%                | 6.8 M             |\n| Moderate    | 129   | 32.3%                | 9.0 M             |\n| High        | 94    | 23.6%                | 8.6 M             |\n| Critical    | 22    | 5.5%                 | 2.1 M             |\n| **Total**   | **399** | **100.0%**          | **~26.5 M**       |\n\n**Expected loss** is calculated as:\n```\nExpected Loss = Outstanding Balance * (1 - Payment Rate (%) / 100)\n```\n- The **Moderate** and **High** tiers together comprise 55.9% of the high-risk group and account for 66.0% of the expected loss.\n\n## 7. Risk-Warning Model for the Next 6 Months\n\nBecause the `quickbooks__cashflow_forecast` table contains no forecast data, we built a simple but conservative warning model based on current outstanding balances and payment-rate deterioration.\n\n1. **Loss-Projection Formula:**\n```\nProjected Loss (next 6 months) =\n    Sum [Outstanding Balance * (1 - (Payment Rate - Trend Deterioration Rate) / 100)]\n```\n   - Trend deterioration rate = average monthly decline observed in the last 12 months = -1.92 pp per month.\n   - Over 6 months, the payment rate could drop by another 11.5 pp.\n\n   Applying this to the high-risk cohort yields an **additional loss exposure of approx $5.3 M** over the next six months.\n\n2. **Risk-Warning Triggers:**\n   - A further drop in collection rate for **three consecutive months**.\n   - Any reduction in the credit score of customers already in Critical or High tiers.\n   - An increase in the outstanding balance of any customer already flagged as high-risk.\n\n## 8. Tiered Customer-Management & Risk-Control Strategies\n\n| Tier        | Recommended Actions                                                                                                                                             |\n|-------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Critical** (Risk Score >= 70) | * Immediate suspension of new credit<br>* Outsource collection to a specialized agency<br>* Require partial payment before shipment of new orders                 |\n| **High** (Risk Score 60-70)     | * Reduce credit limits by 50%<br>* Increase follow-up frequency from weekly to daily<br>* Offer small-discount incentives for early payment<br>* Monitor monthly financial statements |\n| **Moderate** (Risk Score 50-60) | * Introduce a formal payment-plan option<br>* Regular (weekly) payment reminders<br>* Review credit limits; freeze any increases until performance improves          |\n| **Low** (Risk Score < 50)       | * Standard monitoring and quarterly reviews.<br>* Encourage automated payment methods to improve payment timeliness.<br>* Provide early-payment discount options. |\n\n**Cross-Cutting Initiatives:**\n1. **Strengthen onboarding checks:** Incorporate the composite risk score into new-customer credit evaluations.\n2. **Dynamic credit limits:** Tie credit limits to the risk score, automatically adjusting as the score changes.\n3. **Collection-rate dashboard:** Create a real-time dashboard that highlights month-on-month drops in collection rate, with alerts after two consecutive monthly declines.\n\n## 9. Conclusion\n\n- 399 customers (14.3% of the base) are classified as high-risk, carrying an expected loss of $26.5 M.\n- The collection rate has shown repeated three-month deterioration cycles, indicating an underlying structural issue in collection practices.\n- High-risk customers exhibit similar operational and profitability profiles to the normal cohort, suggesting that payment-risk is independent of business volume or longevity.\n- Segmentation into four risk tiers enables targeted, tiered intervention strategies that can mitigate future losses while preserving revenue from lower-risk tiers.\n\n**Immediate priority:** Act on the 22 Critical-risk customers to prevent further losses, and implement the tiered management framework for the remaining high-risk groups. Concurrently, address the systemic collection-rate decline through process improvements and enhanced monitoring.\n\n*Report generated by Data Analytics Team*')\"")
